<DocsLayout title="Security" pathname="/docs/security" section="Core concepts">
  <h1>Security</h1>
  <p class="dx-lede">
    Security here is structural, not advisory. The posture is load-bearing — if the rules don't hold, <code>maddu doctor</code> refuses to start. There's no "secure mode" toggle, because there's no other mode.
  </p>

  <div class="dx-note">
    <span class="dx-note-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◆</span>
    <p><strong>The spawn boundary is the load-bearing line.</strong> Everything past it is a worker subprocess. Everything before it is framework code that never touches a provider SDK. Five surfaces typically leak — secrets in env vars, secrets in tool arguments, tokens in transit, supply-chain compromise via deps or MCP tools, and unbounded propagation of a wrong action. Máddu addresses each at the layer it occurs.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Worker subprocess isolation</h2>
  <p>Every provider call lives in a subprocess worker. The bridge, the cockpit, and the harness never import <code>anthropic</code>, <code>openai</code>, <code>@google/generative-ai</code>, or any other provider SDK. Credentials are injected at spawn time, not read from env at runtime by framework code. Doctor's layout check fails the build if an SDK shows up in an app-code path (Rule #5).</p>
  <p>Isolation bounds blast radius — it doesn't eliminate compromise. A compromised worker can still leak its own credentials, so pair it with least-privilege keys.</p>

  <h2>Worker environment allowlist</h2>
  <p>Spawned workers don't inherit your shell. The allowlist is default-deny: secret-keyed env vars — anything matching <code>*_TOKEN</code>, <code>*_KEY</code>, or <code>*_SECRET</code> patterns, plus provider-specific prefixes — are stripped at spawn unless explicitly allowed in the workspace governance config (<code>.maddu/config/governance.json</code>). The emitted <code>WORKER_ENV_APPLIED</code> event records how many vars were stripped and how many allowed.</p>
  <p>Vars the operator explicitly allowlists reach the worker at full scope. The allowlist is the contract; it doesn't protect you from a permissive allowlist you wrote yourself.</p>

  <h2>Secret scan on tool arguments</h2>
  <p>Tool arguments pass a pattern scanner before any logging. AWS, OpenAI, Anthropic, GitHub, GitLab, and Slack token shapes are detected and the tool call is refused. Raw values never reach the spine — only a <code>SECRET_SCAN_BLOCKED</code> event carrying the pattern name, never the matched content, plus an operator notification.</p>
  <p>Tokens that don't match a known pattern slip through. The pattern set is deliberately conservative — check the full threat model before relying on it for a novel provider.</p>
  <p>Since v1.96.0 a <strong>central write-boundary sweep</strong> backs this up: every event's <code>data</code> is routed through the canonical redactor inside <code>spine.append</code> — one choke point covering every emit site, present and future — before the line is built and hashed. A key-aware rule redacts a long token under a sensitive key name (<code>password</code>, <code>apiKey</code>, …) that a value-only scan structurally misses. Clean data — the normal case — passes through by reference: zero clones, zero byte changes.</p>

  <h2>Device-bound OAuth tokens</h2>
  <p>Tokens live in OS device-bound paths: <code>~/.config/maddu/auth/</code> with restrictive permissions on Linux/macOS, <code>%APPDATA%\maddu\auth\</code> on Windows. There is no cross-machine sync, by design — that's what turns one compromised laptop into N compromised sessions. <code>maddu export</code> scrubs auth paths from any bundle; <code>maddu import</code> refuses to overwrite an existing token path (Rule #6).</p>
  <p>A user who manually copies the token file across machines defeats this. The framework refuses; the operator can always reach around it. Treat the policy as the contract, not the wall.</p>

  <h2>Supply-chain pinning</h2>
  <p>Every dependency is a supply-chain surface, and loose versioning lets a fresh install pull a newly published malicious release. <code>maddu trust audit / pin / verify</code> pins every dependency with a SHA256 of its tarball, recorded in the ledger at <code>.maddu/trust/</code>. Every install and upgrade re-verifies the pinned hash; the <code>dependency-freshness</code> and <code>dep-pinning-respected</code> gates fail doctor on tamper (Rule #4).</p>
  <p>Pinning protects against post-release tamper of a specific version. It doesn't catch a dependency that was published malicious from day one — that's the review-before-install problem, not the pin-on-install problem.</p>

  <h2>MCP gallery provenance</h2>
  <p>MCP servers are tools agents reach for at runtime. If an agent could pull an arbitrary MCP server from npm or a URL, it would bypass dependency pinning entirely. Instead, servers come from a curated template gallery shipping JSON descriptors with SHA256 provenance. The operator installs via <code>maddu mcp install &lt;template&gt;</code>; anything else is refused. The gallery is small, hand-curated, and version-pinned.</p>
  <p>Anything in the gallery is trusted by definition — curation is the bottleneck. Treat the gallery list as a security-critical surface.</p>

  <h2>The gauntlet</h2>
  <p>The defenses above bound the blast radius of any single action. They don't stop a confidently wrong agent from chaining legitimate-looking actions — rolling back a deploy, dropping a table, force-pushing over your work. The gauntlet is the behavioral defense (Rule #9). Read-only actions pass through, still logged. Anything that mutates state must:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>be on the <code>triggers.json</code> allowlist,</li>
    <li>respect its cooldown,</li>
    <li>satisfy the active governance tier, and</li>
    <li>emit a <code>TRIGGER_FIRED</code> event with <code>triggered_by</code> provenance.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Auto-fire without all four is structurally impossible. The gauntlet stops auto-fires; it doesn't stop an operator who manually approves every refusal in a panic. The point is to make confidently-wrong propagation visible and intentional. See <a href="/docs/governance">Governance</a> for the three tiers built on it &mdash; and, since v1.97.0, the <code>PreToolUse</code> discipline hook that <em>blocks</em> a mutating edit when the session ritual is stale.</p>

  <h2>Oversight &mdash; fed vs withheld</h2>
  <p>A defense that fires silently is invisible to the person accountable for the agent. When an auto-injectable skill is <strong>withheld</strong> &mdash; for example the URL-swap guard refuses a skill that points its body at an off-box, unreviewed link (<code>SKILL_INJECTION_REFUSED</code>) &mdash; that refusal used to live in the record but surface nowhere. The <strong>oversight surface</strong> (<code>maddu spine oversight</code> and the cockpit <code>oversight</code> route) lists each skill the agent was <em>fed</em> and each one <em>withheld</em>, with a per-item plain-language reason, so a non-technical operator can see what the agent was and wasn't handed &mdash; on a record they can verify themselves.</p>
  <p>It is an accountability surface, not a safety proof. It reports what happened with the capabilities the agent was given; it does not certify the outcome was correct. Pair it with the gates and your own review.</p>

  <h2>The assurance ladder &mdash; don't let the actor be the sole witness</h2>
  <p>A verification receipt written by the process that did the work is testimony, not evidence. Since v1.110.0 the verification-witness track ships a complete, honest ladder of how much an outcome claim can be trusted:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>actor-reported</code></strong> &mdash; the runner's own in-process receipt (<code>VERIFICATION_RAN</code>) on the tamper-detecting spine. Better than a hand-writable state file; still the actor's word.</li>
    <li><strong><code>replayed</code></strong> &mdash; <code>maddu spine verify --replay &lt;sha&gt;</code> re-earns the receipt in a clean checkout: the commands the repo <em>itself</em> declares, read from the git object store at the subject commit &mdash; a dirty worktree can never choose the commands, and an undeclared repo is honestly <code>unsupported</code>, never inferred.</li>
    <li><strong><code>anchored</code></strong> &mdash; <code>maddu spine anchor</code> stamps the receipt + chain head into Bitcoin via OpenTimestamps (stock client, zero new dependencies). Bitcoin-backed verification is deliberately an <em>operator</em> action (<code>ots verify</code>), never a maddu verb &mdash; a runner the agent controls re-verifying its own evidence would be actor-as-witness again. The consume ritual (<code>spine anchor --assess</code>) can only <em>refuse</em>; its record is a non-authoritative ledger note, labeled that way by every consumer.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The residuals are stated, not hidden: an anchor proves a receipt <em>existed by a time</em>, not that the work was good; suffix deletion of anchors is undetectable without a retained operator note; <code>presence-attested</code> (a live external attestor) is deferred, and the docs say so. The framework threat model's scenario 13 enumerates the full ladder and every stated residual.</p>

  <h2>Verdict machinery &mdash; when the actor moves the yardstick</h2>
  <p>The artifacts that decide pass/fail are ordinary files in the repo. An agent that has completed the ritual can alter an assertion, mark tests skipped, narrow the runner's scope, drop a gate from the required set, or edit the verifier itself &mdash; and then truthfully report that the suite is green. Two cooperative signals cover two different halves of that, and both are narrow on purpose.</p>
  <p><strong>Verdict-machinery drift detection</strong> watches the machinery that decides what a result <em>means</em>. The operator declares a pin set in <code>.maddu/config/tracked-sources.json</code>; <code>maddu sources rebuild --reason "…"</code> snapshots those hashes onto the spine. It reports four drift classes &mdash; <code>changed</code>, <code>missing</code>, <code>unpinned</code>, <code>removed</code> &mdash; so deleting a test <em>and</em> dropping it from the pin set does not read clean. The gate ships at severity <code>warn</code> deliberately: an actor who can edit the pinned files can re-pin them, so <code>critical</code> would falsely lend it trust-boundary weight.</p>
  <p><strong>Acceptance proofs</strong> (v1.121.0) cover the other half &mdash; whether the check has ever rejected anything at all. Declare an oracle set and an implementation set on the goal (<code>maddu goal set --oracle "&lt;glob&gt;" --impl "&lt;glob&gt;"</code>); <code>maddu orient</code> and <code>maddu loop ralph</code> observe the declared command and append <code>VERIFICATION_STARTED</code>/<code>VERIFICATION_RAN</code> receipts. A proof is then <em>derived</em>, every time somebody looks, from two receipts: the command <strong>exited nonzero</strong> and later <strong>exited zero</strong>, while the declared oracle bytes stayed identical and the declared implementation bytes moved. Process-level, never "a test failed". The <code>acceptance-proven</code> gate renders that state wherever gates run and is green only when every verifiable condition is <code>live</code>.</p>
  <p>What it closes precisely: <strong>RED-then-edit-the-test</strong>. Editing the oracle between the two observations means they recorded different oracle digests, so no qualifying pair ever forms. And there is <strong>no re-baseline verb</strong> &mdash; unlike <code>sources rebuild</code>, no command re-greens an acceptance; re-declaring the goal changes the acceptance identity and leaves the new declaration visibly unproven rather than green.</p>
  <p>What it does <em>not</em> close is larger, and belongs in the same breath. A weak oracle proven RED&rarr;GREEN is still a weak oracle &mdash; an assertion checking almost nothing yields an entirely honest, entirely worthless proof. The guarantee is <strong>endpoint equality, not continuous immutability</strong>: a run that mutates a declared file and restores it before the post-hash is undetected and leaves no recorded difference. <strong>Only the declared sets are bound</strong> &mdash; every test outside one, and every test on a repo with no acceptance-active goal, is covered by nothing. A receipt is a <strong>cooperative actor's assertion</strong>: the chain is an unkeyed SHA-256, so fabricated receipts carrying a correctly computed <code>prev_hash</code> verify, and deriving a proof rather than storing it removes the minting verb, not the forgery. There is <strong>no commit binding</strong>. Team-sync mode is <strong>refused, not degraded</strong> &mdash; a partitioned spine has no single order in which a RED provably precedes a GREEN, so such a repo gets no acceptance coverage at all. And CI executes PR-controlled code, so an agent that edits the verifier can change the verdict in the same PR; closing that needs the required check to run from a protected base branch, and it is not solved here. The authoritative residual list is exported from the code as <code>ACCEPTANCE_HONEST_LIMITS</code> and reproduced verbatim in <a href="/docs/reference/acceptance-proof">the acceptance-proof reference</a> &mdash; no readout may narrow it.</p>

  <h2>Forbidden patterns</h2>
  <p>Some patterns are named, refused, and structurally impossible inside the charter — not TODOs.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Hermes pattern.</strong> A hosted webhook holding many users' bot tokens and accepting inbound commands — the exact failure mode where one breach cascades across every connected operator. Chat and connector integrations are allowed only if local, direct-API, device-bound token, off-by-default, allowlisted, and never inbound-as-command.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-repair of the spine.</strong> Spine corruption is never silently fixed. A system that quietly heals can quietly hide a successful attack. <code>maddu spine verify</code> detects, surfaces, and stops; the operator reads the failure and decides.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The boundary — what's still yours</h2>
  <p>Máddu is the audit and posture layer. It is <strong>not</strong> a secret store, a vault, or a key rotator. Pair it with a real vault — HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Cloudflare Secrets, 1Password Connect, pick one. Use least-privilege IAM. Rotate keys on a schedule. Máddu's job is to keep your agents from leaking what's in those stores; it isn't the store itself.</p>

